All 55+ examples now follow a consistent pattern for displaying the Vulkan profiler UI overlay with proper styling.

### Required Setup in Example Headers (.h)

```cpp
#pragma once

#include "Sample.h"
#include <Urho3D/Graphics/ProfilerUI.h>

class YourExample : public Sample
{
    // ... class definition ...

private:
    /// Profiler UI overlay
    SharedPtr<ProfilerUI> profilerUI_;
};
```

### Required Setup in Start() Method

```cpp
void YourExample::Start()
{
    // Execute base class startup
    Sample::Start();

    // Load UI style for ProfilerUI (must be before creating UI elements)
    auto* cache = GetSubsystem<ResourceCache>();
    auto* uiStyle = cache->GetResource<XMLFile>("UI/DefaultStyle.xml");
    GetSubsystem<UI>()->GetRoot()->SetDefaultStyle(uiStyle);

    // ... create scene, UI, setup viewport, subscribe to events ...

    // Set the mouse mode to use in the sample
    Sample::InitMouseMode(MM_FREE);

    // Initialize profiler UI
    auto* graphics = GetSubsystem<Graphics>();
    auto* ui = GetSubsystem<UI>();
    profilerUI_ = new ProfilerUI(context_);
    profilerUI_->Initialize(ui, graphics->GetVulkanProfiler());
    profilerUI_->SetVisible(true);
}
```

### Required Setup in HandleUpdate() Method

```cpp
void YourExample::HandleUpdate(StringHash eventType, VariantMap& eventData)
{
    using namespace Update;
    float timeStep = eventData[P_TIMESTEP].GetFloat();

    // ... your update code ...

    // Update profiler display
    if (profilerUI_)
    {
        GetSubsystem<Graphics>()->GetVulkanProfiler()->RecordFrame(timeStep);
        profilerUI_->Update();
    }
}
```

### Key Points

1. **UI style must be loaded FIRST** - before any UI element creation
2. **ProfilerUI must be initialized LAST** - after InitMouseMode() and all other setup
3. **HandleUpdate must call both RecordFrame() and Update()** - to properly display metrics
4. **All examples use the same pattern** - ensures consistency across the codebase

### Current Status

✅ **Al

Object-based pub/sub. Every Object can send and subscribe to events.

```cpp
// Subscribe to an event
SubscribeToEvent(E_UPDATE, URHO3D_HANDLER(MyClass, HandleUpdate));

// Or with lambda
SubscribeToEvent(E_UPDATE, [this](StringHash, VariantMap& eventData) {
    float dt = eventData[Update::P_TIMESTEP].GetFloat();
});

// Send an event
SendEvent(E_MY_CUSTOM_EVENT, myEventData);
```

Events use StringHash keys for O(1) lookup. VariantMap carries typed payload. Subscribe to a specific sender node with `SubscribeToEvent(senderNode, E_NODECOLLISION, ...)`.

Urho3D uses intrusive reference counting via RefCounted base class.

```cpp
// SharedPtr — owns a reference, prevents deletion
SharedPtr<Node> node(scene_->CreateChild("Foo"));
// refcount increments on copy, decrements on destroy

// WeakPtr — observes without preventing deletion
WeakPtr<Node> weakNode(node);
if (weakNode.Expired())  // safe check
    return;
Node* raw = weakNode.Get();  // may be null
```

Key rules:
- Store SharedPtr for ownership (member variables that keep things alive)
- Store WeakPtr for references that shouldn't prevent cleanup (fishNodes_, cached pointers)
- Never store raw pointers to RefCounted objects long-term — they can be deleted under you
- Get() on WeakPtr returns null if object was deleted — always check

```cpp
// Header: MyComponent.h
#pragma once
#include <Urho3D/Scene/LogicComponent.h>

class MyComponent : public LogicComponent
{
    URHO3D_OBJECT(MyComponent, LogicComponent);
public:
    explicit MyComponent(Context* context);
    static void RegisterObject(Context* context);

    void Start() override;           // called once when added to scene
    void FixedUpdate(float dt) override;  // physics rate
    void Update(float dt) override;       // frame rate

private:
    float speed_{5.0f};
};
```

Register in your Application::Start():
```cpp
MyComponent::RegisterObject(context_);
```

RegisterObject defines serializable attributes:
```cpp
void MyComponent::RegisterObject(Context* context)
{
    context->RegisterFactory<MyComponent>();
    URHO3D_ATTRIBUTE("Speed", float, speed_, 5.0f, AM_DEFAULT);
}
```

Urho3D::HashMap is its own implementation. Different iteration and API:

```cpp
HashMap<unsigned, ServerCreatureAI> creatureAI_;

// Insert
creatureAI_[spawnId] = ai;

// Find
auto it = creatureAI_.Find(spawnId);
if (it != creatureAI_.End())
{
    ServerCreatureAI& ai = it->second_;
    // note: second_ not second
}

// Iterate
for (auto it = creatureAI_.Begin(); it != creatureAI_.End(); ++it)
{
    unsigned key = it->first_;
    // ...
}

// Erase during iteration — DON'T. Collect keys first.
Vector<unsigned> toRemove;
for (auto it = map.Begin(); it != map.End(); ++it)
    if (shouldRemove(it->second_))
        toRemove.Push(it->first_);
for (unsigned k : toRemove)
    map.Erase(k);
```

Key difference from std: `first_` and `second_` with underscore suffix.

SLikeNet (RakNet fork) underneath. Server/client model with scene replication.

```cpp
// Server
auto* network = GetSubsystem<Network>();
network->StartServer(2345);

// Client
network->Connect("localhost", 2345, scene_);

// Custom messages
VectorBuffer buf;
buf.WriteU32(nodeId);
buf.WriteFloat(damage);
connection->SendMessage(MSG_MY_MSG, true, true, buf);
// args: reliable, inOrder, data

// Handle on receiver
SubscribeToEvent(E_NETWORKMESSAGE, ...);
void HandleMsg(StringHash, VariantMap& data) {
    int msgId = data[NetworkMessage::P_MESSAGEID].GetInt();
    auto& buffer = data[NetworkMessage::P_DATA].GetBuffer();
    MemoryBuffer msg(buffer);
    unsigned id = msg.ReadU32();
    float dmg = msg.ReadFloat();
}
```

Scene replication: REPLICATED nodes auto-sync position, Vars, components. LOCAL nodes are invisible to network. Server creates REPLICATED, client reads them.

VectorBuffer write, MemoryBuffer read. Manual pack/unpack:

```cpp
// Write
VectorBuffer buf;
buf.WriteU32(id);           // 4 bytes
buf.WriteU16(count);        // 2 bytes
buf.WriteU8(state);         // 1 byte
buf.WriteFloat(x);          // 4 bytes
buf.WriteByte(std::byte(v)); // 1 byte (careful: std::byte not unsigned char)
buf.WriteString(name);      // length-prefixed

// Read
MemoryBuffer msg(data);
unsigned id = msg.ReadU32();
unsigned short count = msg.ReadU16();
unsigned char state = msg.ReadU8();
float x = msg.ReadFloat();
std::byte b = msg.ReadByte(); // NOT ReadUByte — doesn't exist
String name = msg.ReadString();
```

Gotcha: No WriteUByte/ReadUByte. Use WriteByte(std::byte) and ReadByte(). Cast carefully.

Pattern used throughout GameDB and WorldDB:

```cpp
sqlite3_stmt* stmt = nullptr;
int rc = sqlite3_prepare_v2(db_,
    "SELECT id, name FROM items WHERE category = ?",
    -1, &stmt, nullptr);
if (rc != SQLITE_OK) return result;

sqlite3_bind_text(stmt, 1, category.CString(), -1, SQLITE_TRANSIENT);
// bind_int for integers, bind_double for floats

while (sqlite3_step(stmt) == SQLITE_ROW)
{
    int id = sqlite3_column_int(stmt, 0);
    const char* name = (const char*)sqlite3_column_text(stmt, 1);
    // ...
}
sqlite3_finalize(stmt);  // ALWAYS finalize
```

Rules:
- Always use prepared statements, never string concat (SQL injection)
- Always finalize, even on error paths
- Use SQLITE_TRANSIENT for String binds (copies the data)
- Wrap bulk inserts in BEGIN/COMMIT for 100x speedup
- Check `sqlite3_changes(db_)` after INSERT/UPDATE to verify rows affected

Data layout determines performance more than algorithm choice.

```cpp
// BAD: pointer-chasing through heap-allocated objects
for (auto it = linkedList.begin(); it != linkedList.end(); ++it)
    sum += (*it)->value;  // cache miss every node

// GOOD: contiguous array, linear scan
Vector<float> values;  // all data in one allocation
for (unsigned i = 0; i < values.Size(); ++i)
    sum += values[i];  // prefetcher loves this

// Structure of Arrays vs Array of Structures
// AoS: struct { float x, y, z, w; } particles[1000];
//   — wastes cache if you only need x,y,z
// SoA: float px[1000], py[1000], pz[1000];
//   — perfect cache utilization per pass
```

Urho3D's HashMap is NOT cache-friendly (linked buckets). For hot loops, extract data into a Vector first, process linearly, write back. The spatial hash classes (FishSpatialHash, LandAnimalSpatialHash) exist because scene graph queries are O(n) cache-hostile.

Modern CPUs speculatively execute past branches. Wrong guess = pipeline flush = ~15 cycle penalty.

```cpp
// BAD: unpredictable branch in hot loop
for (int i = 0; i < N; ++i)
    if (data[i] > threshold)  // random data = 50% misprediction
        sum += data[i];

// BETTER: branchless
for (int i = 0; i < N; ++i)
    sum += data[i] * (data[i] > threshold);  // multiply by 0 or 1

// BEST: sort first, then the branch becomes predictable
// First half: all < threshold (always false)
// Second half: all > threshold (always true)
// Predictor learns the pattern, near-zero misprediction
```

Where this applies in games:
- Visibility culling loops (sort by distance, early-out becomes predictable)
- Entity type checks (group by type, don't interleave)
- The `isHuman` checks in PickCreatureTask — all humans grouped together = predictable branch

SIMD (SSE/AVX) processes 4/8/16 values per instruction but requires aligned data.

```cpp
// 16-byte aligned allocation for SSE
alignas(16) float positions[4];

// SSE intrinsics — 4 floats at once
#include <xmmintrin.h>
__m128 a = _mm_load_ps(positions);      // aligned load
__m128 b = _mm_set1_ps(2.0f);           // broadcast scalar
__m128 c = _mm_mul_ps(a, b);            // 4 multiplies in 1 cycle
_mm_store_ps(positions, c);              // aligned store

// Urho3D Vector3 is NOT aligned (12 bytes, 3 floats)
// For SIMD, pad to Vector4 (16 bytes) or use SoA layout
```

Practical rule: if you're processing thousands of positions/velocities per frame, SoA + SIMD = 4-8x speedup. For < 100 entities, the overhead isn't worth it. The spatial hash approach (bin + linear scan) is better than brute-force SIMD for large entity counts because it eliminates most comparisons entirely.

Mutex: OS kernel transition (~1000 cycles on contention). Fine for low-frequency ops.

```cpp
std::mutex mtx;
{
    std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mtx);
    sharedData = newValue;
}
```

Atomic: hardware CAS instruction (~10 cycles). Good for counters, flags.

```cpp
std::atomic<int> counter{0};
counter.fetch_add(1, std::memory_order_relaxed); // no ordering needed

// For flags:
std::atomic<bool> ready{false};
ready.store(true, std::memory_order_release);  // writer
if (ready.load(std::memory_order_acquire))     // reader
    useTheData();
```

In this codebase:
- YukiHoho uses `std::mutex inferenceMutex_` for the LLM thread — correct, inference is slow anyway
- IPC uses `flock()` file locks for cross-process safety
- Urho3D's main loop is single-threaded (no locks needed for scene/AI)
- WorkQueue exists for parallel jobs but game logic stays on main thread

64 bytes on modern x86. The smallest unit of data the CPU moves between cache and RAM.

When you read `array[0]`, the CPU fetches bytes 0-63 into L1 cache. Reading `array[1]` through `array[15]` (assuming 4-byte floats) is essentially free — already in cache.

False sharing: two threads writing to different variables that share a cache line. Both CPUs fight over ownership, bouncing the line between L1 caches at ~100 cycles per bounce.

```cpp
// BAD: false sharing
struct ThreadData {
    int counterA;  // thread 1 writes
    int counterB;  // thread 2 writes — same cache line!
};

// GOOD: pad to separate cache lines
struct alignas(64) ThreadData {
    int counterA;
    char pad[60];
    int counterB;  // different cache line
};
```

Practical: Urho3D is mostly single-threaded for game logic. False sharing only matters in the WorkQueue and the llama.cpp inference thread.

BFS flood fill on a 2D grid. Classic connected-component labeling.

```cpp
// Water mask: true if heightmap pixel is below water level
Vector<bool> isWater(width * height);
Vector<int> bodyMap(width * height, -1);  // -1 = unvisited

Vector<int> queue;  // BFS queue (stack works too for DFS)

for (int z = 0; z < height; ++z)
    for (int x = 0; x < width; ++x)
    {
        int idx = z * width + x;
        if (!isWater[idx] || bodyMap[idx] >= 0)
            continue;

        // New connected component
        int bodyId = nextBody++;
        queue.Push(idx);
        bodyMap[idx] = bodyId;

        while (!queue.Empty())
        {
            int ci = queue.Back(); queue.Pop();  // BFS front or DFS back
            int cx = ci % width, cz = ci / width;

            // 4-connected neighbors
            for (int n = 0; n < 4; ++n)
            {
                int nx = cx + dx[n], nz = cz + dz[n];
                if (nx < 0 || nx >= width || nz < 0 || nz >= height)
                    continue;
                int ni = nz * width + nx;
                if (isWater[ni] && bodyMap[ni] < 0)
                {
                    bodyMap[ni] = bodyId;
                    queue.Push(ni);
                }
            }
        }
    }
```

Complexity: O(pixels) — every pixel visited exactly once. On 1025x1025 = ~1M pixels, runs in milliseconds. One-time startup cost.

```cpp
FILE* pipe = popen("ls -la", "r");
if (pipe) {
    char buf[256];
    while (fgets(buf, sizeof(buf), pipe))
        result.Append(buf, strlen(buf));
    int status = pclose(pipe);
    // WEXITSTATUS(status) for exit code
}
```

popen = fork + exec + pipe. Creates a child process with stdout piped back to parent. Used in YukiHoho for !sh commands and workboard integration.

Tradeoffs:
- Simple API, works everywhere on POSIX
- Blocks the calling thread (fine for Yuki — inference is already async)
- No stdin control (use pipe/fork/exec for bidirectional)
- Shell injection risk if command contains user input — sanitize or don't use user strings

Alternative: Urho3D's FileSystem::SystemRun() — but it doesn't capture output. popen is the right choice when you need the output.

All assets go through ResourceCache:

```cpp
auto* cache = GetSubsystem<ResourceCache>();

// Load model
auto* model = cache->GetResource<Model>("Models/Box.mdl");

// Load with null-safety (won't crash if missing)
auto* mat = cache->GetResource<Material>("Materials/Stone.xml", false);
if (!mat)
    URHO3D_LOGWARNING("Missing material");

// Resources are cached — second call returns same pointer
// No manual memory management needed
```

Resource paths are relative to resource directories (bin/Data/, bin/CoreData/). ResourceCache searches all registered directories.

NEVER use fopen/std::ifstream for game assets. Always use ResourceCache + Urho3D File class. The engine handles cross-platform paths, pack files, and caching.

x86-64 has 16 general-purpose registers (rax-r15) and 16 SSE/AVX registers (xmm0-xmm15). Every variable that lives in a register is accessed in 0 cycles. Every variable that spills to stack costs a load (~4 cycles L1 hit).

What causes register pressure:
- Too many live variables in one scope
- Large structs passed by value (each member needs a register)
- Calling conventions: rdi, rsi, rdx, rcx, r8, r9 for args, rax for return. Caller-saved = you lose them across function calls.

```cpp
// BAD: 8 live variables at once, compiler spills
for (int i = 0; i < N; ++i) {
    float x = pos[i].x, y = pos[i].y, z = pos[i].z;
    float vx = vel[i].x, vy = vel[i].y, vz = vel[i].z;
    float ax = acc[i].x, ay = acc[i].y; // spilling now
    // ...
}

// BETTER: process one axis at a time (fewer live vars)
for (int i = 0; i < N; ++i) px[i] += vx[i] * dt;
for (int i = 0; i < N; ++i) py[i] += vy[i] * dt;
for (int i = 0; i < N; ++i) pz[i] += vz[i] * dt;
```

SoA layout wins again — each loop body uses 3-4 registers instead of 9.

Stack: subtract from rsp, done. ~1 cycle. Automatically freed on scope exit.

```cpp
float localArray[64];  // stack — instant, cache-hot
```

Heap: malloc/new calls into allocator, may syscall (mmap/brk). 50-10000 cycles.

```cpp
float* heapArray = new float[64];  // heap — slow, may cache-miss
delete[] heapArray;  // must remember to free
```

Urho3D containers (Vector, HashMap, String) are heap-allocated internally. Each push_back may reallocate.

Hot-path rule: avoid heap allocation in per-frame code.

```cpp
// BAD: allocates every frame
void Update(float dt) {
    Vector<Node*> nearby;  // heap alloc in constructor
    scene_->GetChildren(nearby);  // more allocs
}

// GOOD: reuse member
class MyComponent {
    Vector<Node*> nearbyCache_;  // allocated once, reused
    void Update(float dt) {
        nearbyCache_.Clear();  // no dealloc, just reset size
        // ...
    }
};
```

The Urho3D pattern: member Vector<> cleared each frame is cheaper than local Vector<> constructed each frame.

C++11 move: transfer ownership instead of copying. Steal the guts.

```cpp
// String has a char* buffer and a size
String a = "hello world";  // a owns the buffer
String b = std::move(a);   // b steals a's buffer. a is now empty.
// Zero copies. Just pointer swap.

// Return value: compiler applies RVO (return value optimization)
// or implicit move. Don't std::move the return — it prevents RVO.
String MakeName() {
    String s = "NPC_" + String(id);
    return s;  // RVO or implicit move. NOT: return std::move(s);
}
```

Move constructor:
```cpp
class Buffer {
    char* data_;
    size_t size_;
public:
    Buffer(Buffer&& other) noexcept  // noexcept enables optimizations
        : data_(other.data_), size_(other.size_)
    {
        other.data_ = nullptr;  // leave source in valid empty state
        other.size_ = 0;
    }
};
```

Rule: move is a performance optimization, not a semantic change. After move, the source object must still be destructible and assignable — just empty.

CAS is the fundamental building block of lock-free programming.

```cpp
// Pseudocode:
bool CAS(int* addr, int expected, int desired) {
    // Atomic — entire operation is indivisible
    if (*addr == expected) {
        *addr = desired;
        return true;   // success
    }
    return false;      // someone else changed it
}
```

On x86:
```asm
lock cmpxchg [rdi], esi
; rdi = address, eax = expected, esi = desired
; if [rdi] == eax: [rdi] = esi, ZF=1
; else: eax = [rdi], ZF=0
; lock prefix = bus lock / cache lock — atomic across all cores
```

The `lock` prefix prevents any other core from touching that cache line during the operation. Cost: ~10-20 cycles uncontended, 100+ cycles contended (cores fighting over the cache line).

C++ usage:
```cpp
std::atomic<int> counter{0};
int old = counter.load();
while (!counter.compare_exchange_weak(old, old + 1))
    ;  // retry on failure
// Or just: counter.fetch_add(1);
```

Lock-free queues, counters, and flags all build on CAS. Mutex uses it internally (futex = CAS + syscall fallback on contention).

Templates = compile-time code generation. The compiler stamps out specialized versions.

```cpp
// Function template — compiler generates one per type used
template <typename T>
T Max(T a, T b) { return a > b ? a : b; }
// Max<int>, Max<float> are separate functions — no virtual, no overhead

// SFINAE — Substitution Failure Is Not An Error
template <typename T>
typename std::enable_if<std::is_integral<T>::value, T>::type
AlignUp(T value, T alignment) {
    return (value + alignment - 1) & ~(alignment - 1);
}
// Only compiles for integer types. Float version would be a different overload.

// constexpr — evaluate at compile time
constexpr int FibonacciCE(int n) {
    return n <= 1 ? n : FibonacciCE(n-1) + FibonacciCE(n-2);
}
static_assert(FibonacciCE(10) == 55);  // computed at compile time, zero runtime cost
```

Urho3D uses templates sparingly — mostly for GetSubsystem<T>(), SharedPtr<T>, and container types. The codebase prefers runtime polymorphism (virtual) over template metaprogramming. That's a style choice — templates bloat binary size, virtuals cost cycles.

A render pass describes what you're drawing to and how.

```cpp
// Attachments: what images are involved
// - color attachment (the framebuffer you see)
// - depth attachment (Z-buffer)
// - G-buffer attachments (deferred: position, normal, albedo, specular)

VkRenderPassBeginInfo beginInfo{};
beginInfo.renderPass = renderPass;
beginInfo.framebuffer = framebuffer;  // binds the actual images
beginInfo.renderArea = {{0,0}, extent};
beginInfo.clearValueCount = 2;
beginInfo.pClearValues = clearValues;  // clear color + depth

vkCmdBeginRenderPass(cmd, &beginInfo, VK_SUBPASS_CONTENTS_INLINE);
// Draw calls go here
vkCmdEndRenderPass(cmd);
```

Subpasses: multiple passes over the same framebuffer without flushing. Deferred rendering uses this — geometry pass writes G-buffer, lighting pass reads it. Input attachments let the lighting pass read the G-buffer without a texture sample (tile-based GPUs love this).

Urho3D Vulkan backend: VulkanGraphicsImpl creates render passes with configurable attachments. Phase 35 added multi-subpass support for deferred rendering.

Three levels of sync:

**Fences** — CPU waits for GPU.
```cpp
vkQueueSubmit(queue, 1, &submitInfo, fence);
vkWaitForFences(device, 1, &fence, VK_TRUE, UINT64_MAX);
// CPU blocks until GPU finishes this submission
```
Used for frame pipelining — wait for frame N-2 before reusing its command buffer.

**Semaphores** — GPU waits for GPU.
```cpp
// Image acquired → render → present
submitInfo.waitSemaphoreCount = 1;
submitInfo.pWaitSemaphores = &imageAvailableSemaphore;  // wait for swapchain
submitInfo.signalSemaphoreCount = 1;
submitInfo.pSignalSemaphores = &renderFinishedSemaphore; // signal when done

presentInfo.waitSemaphoreCount = 1;
presentInfo.pWaitSemaphores = &renderFinishedSemaphore;  // present waits for render
```

**Pipeline barriers** — within a command buffer, order memory access.
```cpp
VkImageMemoryBarrier barrier{};
barrier.oldLayout = VK_IMAGE_LAYOUT_COLOR_ATTACHMENT_OPTIMAL;
barrier.newLayout = VK_IMAGE_LAYOUT_SHADER_READ_ONLY_OPTIMAL;
// Transition G-buffer from write to read between subpasses
vkCmdPipelineBarrier(cmd, srcStage, dstStage, 0, 0, nullptr, 0, nullptr, 1, &barrier);
```

Rule: Vulkan does NOTHING implicitly. Every dependency must be explicit. Miss one barrier → GPU reads stale data → corruption that only shows on some drivers.

Vulkan Memory Allocator (AMD GPUOpen). Vulkan requires manual memory management — you allocate device memory blocks, then suballocate buffers and images from those blocks.

Without VMA:
```cpp
// Allocate memory for a buffer: 4 API calls, choose memory type manually
vkCreateBuffer(device, &bufferInfo, nullptr, &buffer);
vkGetBufferMemoryRequirements(device, buffer, &memReqs);
// Find memory type that's HOST_VISIBLE + HOST_COHERENT...
vkAllocateMemory(device, &allocInfo, nullptr, &memory);
vkBindBufferMemory(device, buffer, memory, 0);
```

With VMA:
```cpp
VmaAllocationCreateInfo allocInfo{};
allocInfo.usage = VMA_MEMORY_USAGE_AUTO;
VmaAllocation allocation;
vmaCreateBuffer(allocator, &bufferInfo, &allocInfo, &buffer, &allocation, nullptr);
// Done. VMA picks the right memory type, suballocates from a pool.
```

VMA handles:
- Memory type selection (GPU-local vs CPU-visible)
- Suballocation (don't waste a 256MB block on a 64-byte uniform buffer)
- Defragmentation
- Budget tracking

Urho3D's VulkanGraphicsImpl creates the VMA allocator at init. All buffer and image allocations go through it.

Programs that run on every vertex/pixel in parallel on the GPU.

**Vertex shader**: transforms each vertex from model space to screen space.
```glsl
void VS() {
    gl_Position = cViewProj * cModel * vec4(iPos, 1.0);  // model → world → clip
    vTexCoord = iTexCoord;
    vNormal = normalize((cModel * vec4(iNormal, 0.0)).xyz);
}
```

**Fragment/pixel shader**: computes the color of each pixel.
```glsl
void PS() {
    vec4 albedo = texture(sDiffMap, vTexCoord);
    float NdotL = max(dot(vNormal, cLightDir), 0.0);
    gl_FragColor = albedo * (cAmbientColor + NdotL * cLightColor);
}
```

GPU runs thousands of these in parallel — one per vertex, one per pixel. No branches, no pointers, no dynamic allocation. Pure math.

Urho3D shaders:
- GLSL files in `bin/CoreData/Shaders/GLSL/`
- Compiled to SPIR-V for Vulkan via shaderc/glslang
- Shader parameters set from C++: `graphics->SetShaderParameter("MatDiffColor", color)`
- Technique XML selects which shader + pass combination to use

Divide world space into a grid. Each cell holds a list of entities in that region. Neighbor query = check 9 cells (2D) or 27 cells (3D) instead of all entities.

```cpp
struct SpatialHash {
    float cellSize;
    HashMap<uint64_t, Vector<unsigned>> cells;

    uint64_t Hash(float x, float z) {
        int cx = (int)floor(x / cellSize);
        int cz = (int)floor(z / cellSize);
        return ((uint64_t)(unsigned)cx << 32) | (unsigned)cz;
    }

    void Insert(unsigned id, float x, float z) {
        cells[Hash(x, z)].Push(id);
    }

    void QueryRadius(float x, float z, float radius, Vector<unsigned>& out) {
        int minCX = (int)floor((x - radius) / cellSize);
        int maxCX = (int)floor((x + radius) / cellSize);
        int minCZ = (int)floor((z - radius) / cellSize);
        int maxCZ = (int)floor((z + radius) / cellSize);
        for (int cx = minCX; cx <= maxCX; ++cx)
            for (int cz = minCZ; cz <= maxCZ; ++cz) {
                auto it = cells.Find(Hash(cx, cz));
                if (it != cells.End())
                    for (unsigned id : it->second_)
                        out.Push(id);
            }
    }
};
```

Complexity: O(1) insert, O(k) query where k = entities in nearby cells. vs O(n) brute force.

FishSpatialHash and LandAnimalSpatialHash in this codebase use this exact pattern. Rebuilt every frame from current positions. cellSize chosen to match typical query radius.

Fixed-size circular array. Head writes, tail reads. When head wraps around, it overwrites the oldest data.

```cpp
template <typename T, int N>
struct RingBuffer {
    T data[N];
    int head{0}, count{0};

    void Push(const T& value) {
        data[head] = value;
        head = (head + 1) % N;
        if (count < N) ++count;
    }

    const T& operator[](int i) const {
        // i=0 is oldest, i=count-1 is newest
        return data[(head - count + i + N) % N];
    }
};
```

Zero allocation after construction. No pointer chasing. Cache-friendly linear access.

Used in this codebase:
- ProfilerTimeline: ring of frame timing data (last N frames)
- ScentRegistry: 16-marker FIFO for death scent positions
- Any "last N events" history

When to use:
- Fixed-size history (don't need to grow)
- Producer/consumer (one writer, one reader — lock-free if single-threaded)
- Streaming data where old values expire naturally

When NOT to use:
- Need random removal (use a HashMap)
- Need to grow (use Vector)
- Multiple writers (need atomic head or mutex)

