A descriptor set is how you tell the GPU which resources (buffers, textures, samplers) a shader can access.

```cpp
// Layout: describes WHAT the shader expects
VkDescriptorSetLayoutBinding binding{};
binding.binding = 0;                           // matches layout(binding=0) in shader
binding.descriptorType = VK_DESCRIPTOR_TYPE_UNIFORM_BUFFER;
binding.descriptorCount = 1;
binding.stageFlags = VK_SHADER_STAGE_VERTEX_BIT;

// Allocate from pool
VkDescriptorSetAllocateInfo allocInfo{};
allocInfo.descriptorPool = pool;
allocInfo.descriptorSetCount = 1;
allocInfo.pSetLayouts = &layout;
vkAllocateDescriptorSets(device, &allocInfo, &set);

// Write: point the descriptor at actual data
VkDescriptorBufferInfo bufInfo{uniformBuffer, 0, sizeof(UBO)};
VkWriteDescriptorSet write{};
write.dstSet = set;
write.dstBinding = 0;
write.descriptorType = VK_DESCRIPTOR_TYPE_UNIFORM_BUFFER;
write.descriptorCount = 1;
write.pBufferInfo = &bufInfo;
vkUpdateDescriptorSets(device, 1, &write, 0, nullptr);

// Bind during rendering
vkCmdBindDescriptorSets(cmd, VK_PIPELINE_BIND_POINT_GRAPHICS,
    pipelineLayout, 0, 1, &set, 0, nullptr);
```

Urho3D Vulkan uses 4 descriptor sets: materials, textures, constant buffers, input attachments. Pipeline layouts cache by hash for performance.

**Tracing GC** (Java, C#, Go): periodically scan all reachable objects from roots, free unreachable ones.
- Pro: handles cycles, no per-object overhead
- Con: unpredictable pauses (stop-the-world), higher memory use (deferred cleanup)

**Reference counting** (Urho3D, Swift, COM): each object tracks how many pointers reference it. Freed when count hits zero.
- Pro: deterministic destruction (destructor runs immediately), predictable performance
- Con: cycles leak (A→B→A, both refcount 1, neither freed), per-operation overhead (inc/dec)

Urho3D's approach:
```cpp
class RefCounted {
    int refs_{0};
    int weakRefs_{0};
public:
    void AddRef() { ++refs_; }
    void ReleaseRef() { if (--refs_ == 0) delete this; }
};
```

SharedPtr increments on copy, decrements on destroy. WeakPtr tracks via weak ref counter without preventing deletion.

Cycle handling: Urho3D doesn't. The scene graph is a tree (parent owns children), not a graph. Cycles shouldn't exist. If they do, it's a bug. WeakPtr breaks potential cycles — use it for back-references (child pointing to parent, cached pointers, observer patterns).

For games, reference counting wins: predictable frame times matter more than peak throughput. A GC pause during combat is unacceptable.

