Three schools:

**Exceptions** (throw/catch):
```cpp
try {
    File f = OpenFile(path);  // throws on failure
    auto data = f.ReadAll();
} catch (const FileException& e) {
    Log(e.what());
}
```
- Pro: can't be ignored, clean separation of happy/error path
- Con: overhead, hard to reason about control flow, not used in Urho3D

**Return codes** (Urho3D style):
```cpp
bool ok = worldDB_->Open(dbPath);
if (!ok) {
    LogMessage("Failed to open world DB");
    return;
}
```
- Pro: explicit, zero overhead, predictable
- Con: easy to forget to check, error handling interleaved with logic

**Optional/nullable** (modern C++):
```cpp
std::optional<ItemInfo> item = gameDB_->GetItem(id);
if (!item.has_value()) return;
Use(*item);
```

Urho3D uses return codes + null checks consistently:
- Functions return bool (success/failure) or nullptr
- ResourceCache::GetResource returns nullptr on missing asset
- SQLite operations return SQLITE_OK or error code
- SharedPtr/WeakPtr.Get() returns nullptr if expired

Rule: always check return values. Three build failures on NPC AI Phase 1 came from assuming API calls succeeded. The engine doesn't throw — it returns null or false, and your code crashes later when you dereference the null.

Double-fork pattern. Detach from terminal, survive parent death.

```cpp
pid_t pid = fork();
if (pid > 0) _exit(0);   // parent exits
if (pid < 0) _exit(1);   // fork failed

setsid();                  // new session leader, no controlling terminal

pid = fork();              // fork again
if (pid > 0) _exit(0);    // first child exits
if (pid < 0) _exit(1);

// Now: grandchild process, no terminal, session leader
chdir("/");                // don't hold a directory mount
umask(0);                  // clear file creation mask
close(STDIN_FILENO);       // release stdin/stdout/stderr
close(STDOUT_FILENO);
close(STDERR_FILENO);

// Write PID file
FILE* f = fopen("/tmp/mydaemon.pid", "w");
fprintf(f, "%d", getpid());
fclose(f);

// Now run the daemon loop
while (running) { ... }
```

Why double-fork: first fork detaches from parent. setsid() creates new session. Second fork ensures process is NOT the session leader — prevents it from accidentally acquiring a terminal.

In this project: WorkboardManager uses this pattern. Claudette spawns Manager as a daemon so it outlives Claudette. PID file goes to /tmp/urho_claude/instances/manager.pid.

IPC mechanism that looks like a network socket but uses the filesystem instead of TCP/IP. No network stack overhead.

```cpp
// Server: listen
int fd = socket(AF_UNIX, SOCK_STREAM, 0);
struct sockaddr_un addr{};
addr.sun_family = AF_UNIX;
strncpy(addr.sun_path, "/tmp/my.sock", sizeof(addr.sun_path) - 1);
unlink(addr.sun_path);  // remove stale socket file
bind(fd, (struct sockaddr*)&addr, sizeof(addr));
listen(fd, 5);

// Accept connections
int client = accept(fd, nullptr, nullptr);
char buf[1024];
ssize_t n = read(client, buf, sizeof(buf));
close(client);

// Client: connect
int fd = socket(AF_UNIX, SOCK_STREAM, 0);
struct sockaddr_un addr{};
addr.sun_family = AF_UNIX;
strncpy(addr.sun_path, "/tmp/my.sock", sizeof(addr.sun_path) - 1);
connect(fd, (struct sockaddr*)&addr, sizeof(addr));
write(fd, "hello", 5);
close(fd);
```

Advantages over TCP localhost:
- No TCP overhead (no handshake, no checksums, no congestion control)
- Filesystem permissions control access
- ~2x faster than TCP loopback

This project uses them everywhere: /tmp/urho_claude/tty/*.sock for all IPC between Claudette, WorkboardManager, coders, planner, Yuki.

Fixed sequence of stages that transforms 3D geometry into 2D pixels.

```
Vertex Data → [Vertex Shader] → [Tessellation] → [Geometry Shader]
           → [Rasterizer] → [Fragment Shader] → [Blending] → Framebuffer
```

**Input Assembly**: read vertices and indices from buffers.

**Vertex Shader** (programmable): transform each vertex. Model → world → view → clip space. Also compute normals, texture coords, lighting inputs.

**Tessellation** (optional): subdivide geometry on the GPU. Good for terrain LOD.

**Geometry Shader** (optional): process whole primitives (triangles). Can emit new geometry. Rarely used — performance is poor.

**Rasterizer** (fixed): convert triangles to fragments (potential pixels). Handles clipping, face culling, depth interpolation.

**Fragment Shader** (programmable): compute color of each pixel. Texture sampling, lighting, shadows, effects. Most artistic control lives here.

**Blending** (fixed): combine new fragment with existing framebuffer value. Alpha blending for transparency, additive for glow.

In Vulkan, you create a VkPipeline that locks in the entire configuration:
```cpp
VkGraphicsPipelineCreateInfo pipelineInfo{};
pipelineInfo.stageCount = 2;  // vertex + fragment
pipelineInfo.pStages = shaderStages;
pipelineInfo.pVertexInputState = &vertexInput;
pipelineInfo.pRasterizationState = &rasterizer;
pipelineInfo.pDepthStencilState = &depthStencil;
pipelineInfo.pColorBlendState = &colorBlend;
// ... 15 more fields
vkCreateGraphicsPipelines(device, cache, 1, &pipelineInfo, nullptr, &pipeline);
```

Pipeline state is immutable after creation — changing ANY state (blend mode, cull mode, shader) requires a different pipeline. Urho3D caches pipelines by state hash to avoid recreating them.
